;
; CALL_ROM is used to call resident procedures and functions; (SP) points
; to the name-table offset for the ROM address (.W); it is followed by a
; byte count & concise (type & separator) parameter specification. Code
; continues from the next even address thereafter. When calling a function
; D5 should indicate the returned type expected (unlike in SUPERCHARGE).
;
; This LIGHTNING routine is general-purpose and thus less than optimal.
;
; Threaded calls have a different escape sequence to native ones -
; they invert the sign of their 'required type' to indicate this.
; JSR and RTS are used in native calls, but the JSR passes the parameter
; address and return is to a computed even address after them.
; In threaded calls this address is intercepted and used to set thread
; - return is to the original stacked address, and hence to another bit
; of threaded code... neat, eh?
