;
; If NEXT without FOR occurs in native code, and the FOR was local and
; also in native code, the vector points directly to NOT_FOUND - fine!
; If NEXT without FOR occurs in threaded code the vector points to NOT
; _FOUND_THREAD, which further vectors the CPU into the error handler.
; If a native NEXT tries to call a threaded FOR the vector should be
; treated as an OR instruction; the MOVEQs get things back in sync, so
; we should fall out to  NOT_FOUND. But the machine will CRASH if a
; threaded NEXT with no FOR follows a native LOCAL_FOR declaration.
; Furthermore, unmatched code-type FOR and NEXT statements will always
; crash - the parser should check for this, as run-time checking is
; unacceptably slow; runtime checking & context switching IS used on
; calls, the consequent degredation is considered acceptable then,
; especially as calls and destinations are generally further apart in
; a program (and therefore less likely to match) than FORs and NEXTs.
;
not_found_thread:
0013D0: 8A D4                                            dc.w       $8AD4  ; src L2118: dc.w not_found-lowest
0013D2: 70 00                                            moveq      #$00,d0  ; src L2119: moveq #0,d0
